In article <2bjavg$29a@eff.org>, Carl M. Kadie <kadie@eff.org> wrote:
>And here part of the CERT policy recommendation:
>
>       This system is for the use of authorized users only.
>       Individuals using this computer system without authority, or in
>       excess of their authority, are subject to having all of their
>       activities on this system monitored and recorded by system
>       personnel. (see ref).
>
>So CERT, an organization changed with protecting user privacy, is
>likely the cause of one of the largest threats to user privacy on the
>Net, a threat to the fourth amendment rights of every authorized
>computer user at a state university.

Hold on there, Carl...you didn't quote *all* relevant parts of the CERT
advisory.  Take a look at *this* part of the advisory:

CERT> Therefore, absent clear guidance from the courts, we believe it is
CERT> advisable for system administrators who will be engaged in such
CERT> monitoring to give notice to those who would be subject to monitoring
CERT> that, by using the system, they are expressly consenting to such
CERT> monitoring.  Since it is important that unauthorized intruders be given
CERT> notice, some form of banner notice at the time of signing on to the
CERT> system is required.  Simply providing written notice in advance to only
CERT> authorized users will not be sufficient to place outside hackers on
CERT> notice.

The CERT advisory didn't *advocate* keystroke monitoring *at all*!
Note the phrase "...administrators who will be engaged in such moni-
toring..."  There is nothing in this advisory which endorses such
activity; it was merely a redistribution of a Department of Justice 
memorandum on the legality of such activity.  It only said (essen-
tially), "if you're going to do this, it would be legally prudent to 
advise all users with a login banner."  That's hardly a smoking gun...

CERT didn't tell anyone to start monitoring keystrokes.  They did say:

CERT> The information that follows is based on the Justice Department's advice
CERT> to all federal agencies.  CERT strongly suggests adding a notice banner
CERT> such as the one included below to all systems.  Sites not covered by U.S.
CERT> law should consult their legal counsel.

but, as they say, that is only a suggestion.

The ethics of keystroke monitoring are subject to much debate; I
won't go into them here.  In this matter, though, I think you're
jumping on CERT just a bit heavily.

--Wes

