>From: streak@wv.engin.umich.edu (Ted C Belding)
>
>I resent your implication that it is my *responsibility* to use 
>encryption, just because someone is trying to violate my privacy.  
>Encrypting email may be the *smart* thing to do, but I expect and 
>demand that my unencrypted email be private also.

Let's get our terms straight before we dive any deeper into the issue.

No one can guarantee the *privacy* of email.  By definition, I cannot
know every security hole in the systems I administer; how, then, can 
I give any meaningful guarantee that email will be private?  I can't.
All it takes in one person with a keystroke monitor and a security 
hole that I don't know about, and your work (or email) is no longer
private.

Now, I can give reasonable guarantees that this staff will not examine
email; I can also give a reasonable guarantee that, in the absence of
outright illegality, any email inadvertently perused by staff (in a
bounced message forwarded to the postmaster, for example) will be held
strictly *confidential*.

It does no good to demand privacy when privacy cannot be guaranteed.
I'll stand right beside you in support of *confidentiality* of online
files and email, but don't ask me to guarantee something that cannot
be guaranteed.

--Wes

