In article <CG74x4.8x4@butch.lmsc.lockheed.com> ciemcb@sealion (Carl Becker) writes:
>
>...and the use of PGP or other encryption, while useful,
>is not the solution to the problem.  The problem, IMHO, is not one of 
>encryption (any code can be cracked)....

In 1977, Rivest, Shamir and Adleman issued a challenge--the first person to
successfully decrypt a message enciphered using RSA with a modulus of only
129 digits (very small by today's security standards) would receive a $100
prize (not mention recognition).

To my knowledge, the prize has yet to be claimed.  In late 1991, Robert
Silverman had an article on factoring in CACM.  In that article, he
stated that he hoped to undertake factoring the modulus, using 3000 Sun
workstations at Sun.  (At this point, factoring is the only known attack
for well-chosen RSA keys.)

These days, 150 digits is considered to be the absolute minimum for a secure
RSA modulus.  200 digit moduli are common, and in some cases, 300 digit
moduli are even used.  At this point, factoring is basically an expontential
problem, so there is little hope of factoring these larger moduli anytime
in the near future.  (The only hope would be a *major* advance in
factoring algorithms.)

It's not clear (and certainly not obvious, as you imply) that "any code can
be cracked."


