Carl, I think you're too hard on CERT on this one.  If you remember the facts
behind the policy, the concern was the legality, under apparently conflicting
provisions of the ECPA, of the providers' doing real-time monitoring
("interception") to track down *unauthorized* access (or access beyond
authorization).

The reluctant conclusion among virtually all legal experts who've studied the
subject is that the ECPA does not clearly give the sysadmins, etc., the right
to do interception to protect themselves. Thus the recommendation for the "CERT
Virus" at the initial contact.  Anyone going forward with a session would
presumably have waived any rights in that regard.

I don't remember anyone (in this newsgroup, at least) openly suggesting that
any sysadmin should, or would, routinely monitor authorized users; rather,
until the law is clarified (always a dangerous practice, turning the
legislature loose on something), the advisory was the best solution to a
confounding problem.

