In article <2bp3hf$2ss@eff.org> kadie@eff.org (Carl M. Kadie) writes:
)
)[This is the official Supreme Court summary. Access information for the
)whole decision is enclosed. - Carl]

)  (b)  Whether an environment is ``hostile'' or ``abusive'' can be
)determined only by looking at all the circumstances, which may
)include the frequency of the discriminatory conduct; its severity;
)whether it is physically threatening or humiliating, or a mere
)offensive utterance; and whether it unreasonably interferes with
)an employee's work performance.  The effect on the employee's
)psychological well-being is relevant in determining whether the
)plaintiff actually found the environment abusive.  But while
)psychological harm, like any other relevant factor, may be taken
)into account, no single factor is required.  Pp. 5-6.

If the courts hadn't somehow or other decided that 'hostile and abusive'
environments could consist of posters on the wall(s), I might feel better about
this decision (Jacksonville Shipyards comes to mind as an example of
theis for starters, I'm fairly sure there are others from some general reading
I've done though I can't cite cases).

Of course, it seems like there is a lot of internal politicking going on
this issue anyway - with various district courts coming up with totally
different interpretations of the law, including some that are downright
sexist in proposing a separate 'reasonable man' and 'reasonable woman' standard.
(Ellison vs. Brady).  In that light, I suppose it's encouraging.

I think the justice who complained in a separate opinion that the whole law
was fundamentally far too vague was right on the mark - too bad he didn't
go further, and perhaps suggest that it be thrown out on that basis
('unconstitutionally vague'), or even that the 'hostile environment' test 
wasn't really the 'will of Congress'.

