In article <2bmrt2$j3f@eff.org>, Carl M. Kadie <kadie@eff.org> wrote:
>A critique.
>
>>        ANTI-WATCHDOG programs are prohibited on the VAXcluster.
>
>Mere possession of programs should not be banned. Programs are
>expressive material and should be protected by the Institute's
>commitment to free expression. 


We've never really reached a consensus on this particular issue.
Consider this:

	- My obligation to my users includes connectivity to their systems.
	- Said connectivity is often limited (in our case, by the number
	  of available lines)
	- To increase availability of said lines, I run an "watchdog"
	  which logs out any user who has been idle for more than 20 
	  minutes.
	- Programs which do nothing but defeat the "watchdog" are
	  of no real value in their execution, once the technique has 
	  been proven (I'll yield to the curiosity of a user who wants 
	  to "see if they can cheat it" -- once.)
	- I have no way of knowing if a "busy" user is actually doing
	  something or merely running an "anti-watchdog" program.
	- I have no means of finding such programs, once compiled, other 
	  than a search of user files (which is NOT going to happen) or
	  physical observation of the user's terminal.

Doesn't my obligation to *all* the users outweigh the academic freedom
issue of having the source code lying about?  With password cracking
software, the argument could be made that the excessive CPU usage might
set off some alarms, but that can't be said for a program that just in-
jects a character down the line every few seconds.

>At most the Institute should require
>that banned-from-running programs be disabled so they can't be
>executed accidently.

How, pray tell, would this be accomplished?  Furthermore, how would
such a requirement be enforced *without* violating user privacy?

It's easy to sit back and say that "Possession of source code should
not be restricted" and "Programs are a form of free expression."  It's
quite another thing to balance such an attitude with one's obligations
to *all* users.  

If you've got a solution for this one, I'd *love* to hear it.  It was
basically ignored when last I mentioned it here.

--Wes

