Let me put a few things in context.

First, if we were talking about a policy enacted by a public school, the
"void for vagueness" doctrine would apply.  At least, it did in the
cases of the U. Michigan and U. Wisconsin rules (although the latter rule
was not found to be unconstitutionally vague).  So I presume that the
doctrine applies to "disciplinary" procedures in general (within the
context of a governmental institution).

In general, I use the terminology applicable to a public institution,
even though SU is a private institution.  I just follow it up with statements
about what (I think) it means within the context of a private school.

Within the context of a public institution, we have to look to contractual
issues.  In this case, my analysis is fairly simple, and I believe
reasonable on its face.

   1) The contract has been unilaterally adopted, with the institution
      being the maker of the contract.  Faculty, staff and students must
      take it or leave it.

   2) If the statute is unconstitutionally vague in its definition of
      prohibited conduct (in the context of a public institution), we
      should be able to agree that it is vague, period.

   3) Ambiguities in a contract are generally interpreted against the
      maker of the contract, which in this case is the school.  Therefore,
      it seems very likely that behavior which is not clearly prohibited
      by the policy would not be ruled a violation of the policy (if
      challenged in court).


Generally speaking, I have serious doubts that a court would uphold
disciplinary sanctions based on conduct that was not clearly prohibited
by the policy.  A contractual provision that prohibits conduct it fails
to (adequately) specify seems "unconscionable" to my uneducated eye.


I'd be glad to see any concurring or dissenting opinions.


