This is an open Request For Comments on the alt.* ban, computer privacy
and other issues in the "electronic frontier" addressed to the
community of UVic students, staff, faculty, and system administrators,
as well as those USENET readers in comp.org.eff.talk so that our
dialogue might benefit from their experience.

I would like to give a bit of background to this issue first. 

NOTE: (as I am getting involved after the fact, I have only
unsubstantiated rumour to go on and would GREATLY appreciate somebody
responding to this forum with the correct turn of events. I have
written to the VP Academic (Dr. Sam Scully) inquiring how all of this
occurred and hope to hear back from him soon.)

*Apparently*, some months ago a prof or administrators wife saw some
rude GIF image from the USENET news displayed on one of the screens on
a computer at the University and was offended by it. A series of
knee-jerk reactions led to the entire alt.* hierarchy being made
unavailable using the regular USENET reading software such as rn, nn,
or Nuntius, with very few exceptions. The current policy is now that
users may attain permission to read particular alt.newsgroups if they
can get a letter from any senior administrator (preferably a Dean or
higher) saying that access is required for academic purposes. This
means that many users are unaware of and don't have access to many
other useful groups (and yes, I do include the alt.sex group in the
useful category) Some of the other groups like
alt.comp.acad-freedom.news (computers and academic freedom) and
alt.censorship are of course not accessible by UVic users. 

In article <1993Nov11.033251.4283@sol.uvic.ca> I wrote in uvic.general:

+As you probably know, access to the alt.newsgroups has for the most
+part been banned by the higher ups. I am currently discussing this
with
+the Electronic Frontier Foundation in Washington (they write the
+Computers and Academic Freedoms newsletters) in order to research
+similar cases and relevant Canadian law. 

+If you are at all interested in helping me with this, or just want to
+let me know what you think of the ban, leave a note here, or feel free
+to e-mail me at the address below.


In article <1993Nov12.012406.15297@sol.UVic.CA>
klassen@sol.UVic.CA (Melvin Klassen) responded:

>  - Your status as a "student" at UVic does NOT automatically grant
>    you full access to the Internet.  You get LIMITED computer-access 
>    in order to complete your computer-related assignments.

I apologize for not making my original article
<1993Nov11.033251.4283@sol.uvic.ca> very clear. I NEVER intended to
suggest that users had the right to USENET News under the *current
University computing policy*. I AM however interested in where people
at UVic stand on certain issues such as e-mail privacy, current
computer policies, ideal computer policies, and promotion of and access
to the Internet within the student and faculty population at the
University of Victoria.

What I would like to see is some discussion amongst the students,
system administrators, librarians, faculty, and staff on what we would
like to see this University adopt as computing policy. Perhaps the best
forum for that is a University Senate Committee (the membership of
which should be made up of students, Senate members, library staff, and
computing services staff). 

How do we (the students, staff, etc.) of UVic wish to deal with:

1) Electronic Freedom of speech and expression?
2) E-mail and privacy issues?
3) Due process?
4) Right and responsibilities of the users?

Q.1) Electronic Freedom of speech and expression

I would propose that users at UVic be given free access to the Internet
and all of its resources and newsgroups *directly* with no
restrictions. This was the recent decision at the University of Toronto
when somebody suggested that they censor portions of the newsgroups. At
the University of Manitoba several years ago, the rec.humour.funny and
the alt.sex groups were banned and were re-instated after a University
committee was struck to examine the issues involved. UBC and SFU have
both recently decided to ban portions of the newsgroups for various
reasons as decided by their respective committees or adminstrators. I
am not aware of any backlash or call for review by the students there,
but expect to hear about it soon. At many universities across the US,
various newsgroups were banned and then reinstated after deciding to
treat Internet material in the same manner as library materials.

Most universities already have an agreed policy, usually set up by
their library.  The CLA (Canadian Library Association) policy includes
the following:

"All persons in Canada have the fundamental right, as embodied in the
nation's Bill of Rights and the Canadian Charter of Rights and
Freedoms, to
have access to all expressions of knowledge, creativity and
intellectual
activity, and to express their thoughts publicly.  This right to
intellectual freedom, under the law, is essential to the health and
development of Canadian society [...] It is the responsibility of
libraries to guarantee and facilitate access to all expressions of
knowledge and intellectual activity, including those which some some
elements of society may consider to be unconventional, unpopular or
unacceptable.  To this end, libraries shall acquire and make available
the widest variety of materials."

[reference <1990Dec10.175530.23753@cs.wayne.edu> and
<1990Dec10.193339.5349@zoo.toronto.edu>]

Further,  in pub/academic/faq/policy.best located on ftp.eff.org (a
computer at the office of EFF in Washington) they have quoted the
policy of the University of Wisconsin at Milwaukee which is held up by
the EFF (Electronic Frontier Foundation) as a model policy regarding
USENET news:

"That the same standards and principles of intellectual and academic
freedom developed for university libraries be applied to material
received from the news network... That the same standards of
intellectual and academic freedom developed for faculty and student
publication in traditional media be applied to publication in computer
media."

I would like to see UVic adopt similar policies especially in light of
the nature of USENET news and the internet in general. The material
which UVic is no longer providing to its users through their news
reading software is still available on the internet but it requires
more work to get at it.Banning a newsgroup (refusing to carry it on
your server and allow it to be read directly on campus) does not of
course mean that users have no access to the banned material at all.
All of the materials in question such as the Alt.* newsgroups are still
accessible of course via Gopher, FTP,  and remote login to other
computers, but since the required knowledge is greater, most people
will not bother. It could be termed "Censorship by making it more
difficult than it usually is." A common approach to censoring something
(not necessarily electronic, this of course also applies to library
materials or other information outside of a University context) is to
make it so difficult to access that most people will not bother. This
is the route that UVic has chosen and to the best of my knowledge they
did this without wide consultation involving the library, students, and
other affected parties - consultation which in my opinion should occur
now.

2) E-mail and privacy issues?

Should e-mail and files be private or should system administrators be
allowed to read it or search it if they find it "necessary" for
whatever reason?

3) Due process?

How does the University deal with infractions? Should the punitary
measures be administered automatically by the System Administrators or
should the student be given a hearing first? Should punishment be in
the form of computer privileges being revoked or be based on the same
system which is used for every other infraction on a campus be it
harassment, vandalism, or whatever. 

4) Right and responsibilities of the users?

Some universities give their users rights, while others give them only
a list of "What not to do" and "No, of course your e-mail isn't
private, and Yes we can look in your files if we want to." Should UVic
give all students the "right" to Internet access in general and the
"right" to free expression and involvement with all of the global
citizens of "cyberspace", unfettered by university control? (Do not get
me wrong...I am not suggesting that UVic currently does the opposite, I
am posing these questions for the reader to consider and reply to.  :-)
 )


I have posed these questions to the academic and administrative
community of UVic, and have of course cross-posted it to the
comp.org.eff.talk so that we might get some input from those folks as
well. I would have posted this instead to alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk
... but we don't have access to it :-).

