 CMK> Can someone suggest the minimum disclaimer needed to patch the bug in
 CMK> the ECPA?

 CMK> CERT's example disclaimer is awful. It says (essentially) that if you
 CMK> think you might ever need to capture the keystrokes of an intruder,
 CMK> then you should compel your users to give up their ECPA and (at state
 CMK> universities) 4th Amendment rights.

 CMK> The main problem is the wording of the "example of an appropriate
 CMK> banner":

 CMK>        This system is for the use of authorized users only.
 CMK>        Individuals using this computer system without authority, or in
 CMK>        excess of their authority, are subject to having all of their
 CMK>        activities on this system monitored and recorded by system
 CMK>        personnel.

 CMK>        In the course of monitoring individuals improperly using this
 CMK>        system, or in the course of system maintenance, the activities
 CMK>        of authorized users may also be monitored.

 CMK>        Anyone using this system expressly consents to such monitoring
 CMK>        and is advised that if such monitoring reveals possible
 CMK>        evidence of criminal activity, system personnel may provide the
 CMK>        evidence of such monitoring to law enforcement officials.

I think you don't pay enough attention to the middle paragraph:

       In the course of monitoring individuals improperly using this
       system, or in the course of system maintenance, the activities
       of authorized users may also be monitored.

In my mind this acts as a limitation on the entire notice.  Once monitoring
activities showed the target to have authorized access, all monitoring
activities should cease.

Of course, other people can, as you have, note the broad language which
followed and dismiss the center paragraph.  But generally, words have meaning
and what else does this section mean?  As you know, there is a dearth of court
decisions construing the law, and most of the ones that do turn on
employer-employee law instead of the ECPA.

"For educational purposes only. Not intended as specific legal advice to an 
individual. State laws vary. Consultation, or representation by a local, 
licensed attorney may be necessary."

